# What is committed, and what goes to Google Drive.
#
# The spec forbids committing large binary artifacts and asks for Drive links
# in the README instead. Everything small enough to be useful in review is
# committed, so a grader can read the evidence without opening Drive:
#
#   committed   all code and configs
#               tokenizer .model + .vocab + tokenizer_config.json  (~2 MB)
#               every report table, JSON summary and figure
#               run logs - the evidence behind the numbers
#               the source registry with its probe results
#
#   ignored     raw / clean / dedup / split corpora   (~4 GB)
#               downloaded PDFs                       (~160 MB)
#               spm_input.txt tokenizer training text (~240 MB)
#               tokenized .bin token streams          (~1.1 GB per language)
#               kaggle/ notebooks and upload bundles  (~2.3 GB)
#               Phase 2/3 checkpoints                 (~300 MB per run)
#
# Phase 2 note: train/runs/ holds the checkpoints AND the training log. The log
# is required in the repository, so plot_curves.py copies train.log,
# metrics.jsonl and config.json into <language>/logs/pretrain/<run>/, which is
# committed. One directory per run: pretrain_16k, pretrain_12k.
#
# Note: git does not support trailing comments on a pattern line, so every
# comment here is on its own line.

# ---- corpora: every stage, all large ----
*/data/raw/
# sources withdrawn from the run, kept on disk for reproducibility
*/data/archive/
*/data/clean/
*/data/dedup/
*/data/splits/out/
*.jsonl
*.jsonl.gz

# ---- downloaded source material ----
# *.pdf
# *.pdf.part

# ---- tokenizer build by-products (the .model/.vocab ARE committed) ----
*/tokenizer/spm_input.txt
*/tokenizer/_staging/

# The corpus rule above is a blanket *.jsonl, which also catches the training
# metrics log. That one is evidence, not data: it is what the loss curves are
# drawn from, and the brief asks for training logs in the repository. A few
# hundred KB, so un-ignore it explicitly. Negations must follow the rule they
# override, which is why this sits here and not next to *.jsonl.
!*/logs/pretrain/*/metrics.jsonl

# ---- Phase 2: tokenized token streams (rebuilt by train/prepare_data.py) ----
*/data/tokenized*/
*.bin

# ---- the Kaggle scaffolding ----
# Notebooks, upload/packaging helpers and the 2.3 GB of Datasets they build.
# All of it is machinery for running the training on a hosted GPU, not part of
# the submitted work: the pipeline itself runs from <language>/train and
# <language>/eval, and the README documents those commands directly.
kaggle/

# ---- Phase 2/3 checkpoints and run directories ----
*.pt
*.pth
*.ckpt
*.safetensors
*/model/checkpoints/
*/train/runs/

# ---- LaTeX build intermediates for the Phase 2 report ----
# The PDF is the deliverable and is committed. The .tex is written by
# report/phase2/make_report_pdf.py on every run, so committing it would mean
# keeping two copies of the same thing that can disagree; the script is the
# source. The .aux/.log/.out files are cleaned up automatically unless a build
# fails part-way.
report/phase2/phase2_report.tex
report/phase2/phase2_report.aux
report/phase2/phase2_report.log
report/phase2/phase2_report.out

# ---- not part of the submission ----
# scratch notes
temp.txt
# the assignment brief itself - course material, not work being submitted
LMA_Individual_Project_v1.pdf
# staging for the Hugging Face checkpoint upload: a copy of the model card that
# already lives at huggingface.co/hardik300/LMA_mini_project, plus the script
# that pushed the ten .pt files. Neither is graded evidence.
hf_upload/
local/
# the readable corpora staged for Google Drive
drive_upload/

# ---- Python / env ----
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# ---- OS / editor ----
.DS_Store
*.swp
.vscode/
.idea/


